# The block shift's sign flips with bit depth, and the 10-bit cast was a frame

Host: Apple M4 Pro (8P+4E), macOS 26.5.2, 12 cores.
Base: `main` @ fc120a8, default features, 766/766 dav1d-test-data.
No `nice` on any timed run. No `-C target-cpu=native`.

**The box was NOT idle.** Two other agents held it for this whole session
(`zenav1-svt/identity_run` at ~100% throughout, plus a second rav1d-safe
worktree). Every number below is therefore a **paired per-round ratio**: the
arms are run back to back inside one round with the order rotated, so a round
that ran under load inflates both arms and the ratio survives it. Absolute
ms/frame from these tables are NOT comparable to an idle campaign's; the ratios
are. Every table also prints min/max across rounds, and a claim is only made
where the per-round range clears 1.000.

Harness: `scripts/perf/tshift_ab.sh` + `scripts/perf/tshift_report.py`
(in-process `ms_per_frame` from `bench_ab_decode`, which warms up first).
Every arm built with its own `cargo build --features` invocation and
**sha256-verified distinct** before use — see trap 2 in
`tracker_blockshift_2026-08-08.meta`.

The brief was: make the adaptive shift bit-depth-aware by keying it on pixel
stride or `BD::BPC`, then cut the 10bpc-only `slice_as::<u16>` cost. The second
half held. **The first half's diagnosis did not** — see section 2.


## 1. The headline

`base` = main @ fc120a8. `ship` = this change. Paired per-round ratio, median of
11, in-process timer. **All ten cells md5-identical between arms.** Raw:
`benchmarks/blockshift_bpc_final_2026-08-08.tsv`.

    vector           bpc   t    ratio        range          what moved
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile          8   1   0.9476  [0.9375, 0.9591]   part 1
    v4k_1tile          8   1   0.9426  [0.9381, 0.9608]   part 1
    v4k_8tile_10b     10   1   0.9703  [0.9620, 0.9773]   part 2
    v4k_1tile_10b     10   1   0.9659  [0.9601, 0.9735]   part 2
    v1024              8   1   0.9957  [0.9799, 1.0111]   neutral
    v1024_10b         10   1   0.9164  [0.8888, 0.9227]   part 2
    v4k_8tile          8   8   1.0064  [0.9891, 1.0221]   neutral
    v4k_8tile_10b     10   8   0.9274  [0.9030, 0.9595]   part 2
    v4k_1tile          8   8   0.9962  [0.9889, 0.9998]   neutral
    v4k_1tile_10b     10   8   0.9704  [0.9665, 0.9761]   part 2

No cell is worse. Every 10-bit cell gains 2.9-8.4%; single-threaded 8-bit 4K
gains 5.2-5.7%; the three neutral cells have per-round ranges that straddle
1.000, and their policy is provably unchanged (see 3). The 8-bit multi-thread
column is untouched by construction.


## 2. Part 1 — the brief's diagnosis was wrong, and the ladder says so

The premise handed over was that `block_shift_for` sees only the buffer LENGTH,
that a 10-bit plane is both 2x the bytes and 2x the stride, and that keying on
**pixel stride** or `BD::BPC` would put both depths on the same
picture-rows-per-block and so deliver the 8-bit win without the 10-bit loss.

The first two clauses are true. The conclusion is not, for a reason the
existing code already documents: keying on `len` **already** lands both depths
on the same rows per block (4.27), because the plane's length scales with its
stride. A pixel-stride rule would compute the same 14 / 15 the length rule
computes, and reproduce the regression exactly.

So the ladder had to be mapped rather than assumed. Fixed rungs, t=1, paired
median of 5, against the shipped `BLOCK_SHIFT` of 12
(`benchmarks/blockshift_bpc_ladder_t1_2026-08-08.tsv`):

    vector           bpc    bs13     bs14     bs15     bs16
    ---------------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile          8   0.980    0.958    0.936    0.937
    v4k_8tile_10b     10   1.012    1.059    1.049    1.044

8-bit improves monotonically with the shift. **10-bit gets worse at every rung —
its optimum at t=1 IS the base shift 12.** No rows-per-block matching can
produce that; the answer is not "a different coarse shift for 10-bit", it is
"no coarse shift for 10-bit".

### Is it the bit depth or is it that vector?

Two more pairs, same protocol
(`benchmarks/blockshift_bpc_ladder_pairs_2026-08-08.tsv`):

    vector           bpc    bs14     bs16     ranges vs base
    ----------------------------------------------------------
    v4k_1tile          8   0.958    0.930    separated
    v4k_1tile_10b     10   1.049    1.038    separated
    v1024              8   0.978    0.960    separated at 16
    v1024_10b         10   0.994    0.994    OVERLAP (null)

Two 4K vectors of each depth, differing in tiling AND content, and the sign is
constant within a depth and opposite across it. It is the bit depth.

### Why — the profile, not a story

`/usr/bin/sample`, 40 s, t=1, self-time leaves, share of all samples,
base -> shift 14:

                            8bpc              10bpc
    add_wide + remove_wide  10.65 -> 0.76     5.32 -> 1.23   (what it BUYS)
    add (single-block)      10.80 -> 13.95   11.10 -> 18.30  (what it COSTS)
    net tracker              -6.7 points      +4.0 points

Both depths lose almost all their wide-path traffic, exactly as intended: the
`--features probe-wide` promotion counters drop 1,006,620 -> 72,644 at 8bpc and
622,250 -> 36,510 at 10bpc. But 10-bit had only **half as much wide-path cost to
recover** in the first place, and its single-block fast path pays **over twice**
the penalty for the coarser block. Same mechanism, opposite arithmetic.

That "over twice" is a measured fact, not an explained one — see section 6.

### What shipped

A third monotone latch, `set_pixel_bytes`, beside `set_parallelism` and
`set_tile_concurrency`, called from `decode.rs` next to the tile-split
declaration (both must run before `rav1d_thread_picture_alloc` builds the
tracker). The gate splits:

* **serial** (`shards < SHARDS_CONCURRENT`): adapt iff one byte per pixel.
* **concurrent**: adapt iff `tiles >= 2` — byte-for-byte the shipped rule, and
  deliberately blind to the pixel width, because the t=8 column is a win at
  both depths.

### Adapt UPWARDS ONLY — a regression the first cut shipped

The unclamped serial arm cost **+4.5%** on v1024 at t=1 (paired median of 11,
[1.0227, 1.0596], 11/11 rounds worse) while the 4K planes gained 3%. Cause:
v1024's 8-bit planes are 696,320 bytes, and a rule that aims at a fixed BLOCK
COUNT returns shift **11** there — *finer* than the constant. On a serial decode
the only thing the shift buys is the wide-path promotions a *coarser* block
stops making, so a finer one is the mechanism run backwards. Any buffer under
`2 * N_SHARDS << BLOCK_SHIFT` bytes reaches it.

`shift.max(BLOCK_SHIFT)` on the serial branch only. Attribution
(`benchmarks/blockshift_bpc_attrib_2026-08-08.tsv`, median of 11 vs base):

    cell                  unclamped   clamped
    ---------------------------------------------
    v1024      8bpc t=1     1.0446    1.0013
    v4k_1tile  8bpc t=8     1.0056    0.9951
    v4k_8tile  8bpc t=1     0.9671    0.9584

The v4k_1tile t=8 row is the same bug from the other side: instances built
during decoder construction, before `set_parallelism`, take the serial branch,
and some of them are small.

### And the serial arm aims at half the block count

`BLOCKS_PER_SHARD` is 2 because a concurrent decode balances strided-read
locality against putting every tile worker on one lock. A serial decode has no
second worker, so only the locality half of that trade is left. Halving the
target block count puts the 4K 8-bit plane on shift 15, which is where the
fixed ladder's t=1 column actually bottoms out (0.958 at 14, **0.936 at 15**,
0.937 at 16) — the ratio-2 rule was leaving that on the table.

Measured against the ratio-2 arm, paired median of 9, md5-identical
(`benchmarks/blockshift_bpc_serial_target_2026-08-08.tsv`):

    cell                        ratio         range        rounds better
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile      8bpc t=1    0.9880  [0.9815, 0.9916]        9/9
    v4k_1tile      8bpc t=1    0.9836  [0.9756, 0.9964]        9/9
    v1024          8bpc t=1    1.0034  [0.9792, 1.0097]      neutral
    v4k_8tile      8bpc t=8    0.9968  [0.9832, 1.0345]      neutral
    v4k_8tile_10b 10bpc t=1    0.9976  [0.9886, 1.0053]      neutral

Neutral everywhere the serial-8-bit branch does not fire, which is the shape a
correctly-scoped change should have.


## 3. Liveness — the branch demonstrably fires in a real decode

A perf delta is evidence; this is proof. Temporary `DM_DUMP` instrumentation in
`BorrowTracker::new` (not committed), `--features probe-wide`, 2 iterations:

    arm    vector           t   shift  pxbytes   wide promotions
    ----------------------------------------------------------------
    base   v4k_8tile        1     12       -         1,006,620
    ship   v4k_8tile        1     14       1            72,644
    ship   v4k_8tile        8     14       1                 0
    base   v4k_8tile_10b    1     12       -           622,250
    ship   v4k_8tile_10b    1     12       2           622,250   <- withheld
    ship   v4k_8tile_10b    8     15       2                 0

8-bit serial adapts and sheds 93% of its wide promotions; 10-bit serial is
identical to base; both t=8 columns are the shipped values, confirming the
concurrent branch is untouched.


## 4. Part 2 — the 10bpc cast was a stack frame, and ORDER MATTERED

`slice_as` / `mut_slice_as` cost 2.02% + 2.20% of self time on v4k_8tile_10b at
t=1 and do not exist at 8bpc, where `V == u8` makes every part of them fold to a
bare `index()`.

The release disassembly of `slice_as::<_, u16>` explains it. 100 instructions,
of which the hot path is ~35:

    sub  sp, sp, #0x70        <- 112-byte frame
    stp  x26,x25 / x24,x23 / x22,x21 / x20,x19 / x29,x30   <- 10 spills
    ... ~18 instructions of real work, incl. bl BorrowTracker::add ...
    ldp  x5 / ret

The only writes into that 112-byte frame are the three stores that build a
`CastError` on the cold `.unwrap()` branch. **The success path pays a frame and
ten callee-saved spill/reload pairs to hold a value it never constructs.**

Two candidate fixes, measured as a factorial against `head` (part 1 only),
paired median of 11 (`benchmarks/blockshift_bpc_cast_ab_2026-08-08.tsv`):

    cell                    inline-only   cold-err-only   BOTH
    ---------------------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile_10b   t=1       1.0233         0.9866      0.9652
    v4k_1tile_10b   t=1       1.0272         0.9900      0.9629
    v4k_8tile_10b   t=8       0.9689         0.9761      0.9280
    v4k_8tile        t=8      0.9948         0.9935      1.0038   (8bpc control)

**`#[inline(always)]` on its own is a 2.3-2.7% REGRESSION at t=1** — it inlines
the 112-byte cold frame into every call site. Move the `CastError` out of line
first (a `#[cold] #[inline(never)] cast_slice_failed` that reports the byte
length and address instead; the predicate is untouched, `mut_from_bytes` still
decides) and the frame drops 112 -> 80 bytes, 100 -> 91 instructions. Only then
is forcing the inline a win, and a large one. Both together: the two symbols
disappear from the binary entirely, for +15.9 KB (+0.6%) of text.

If you take one thing from this section: the obvious fix, applied in the obvious
order, measured **worse**, and only a factorial showed why.


## 5. Correctness

* 766/766 `decode_md5_verify`, before and after.
* `examples/md5_inventory` **set-diffed BY NAME** against
  `benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst`: 0 names added, 0
  removed, **0 entries differing** in status or md5. A count alone would hide a
  5-repaired / 5-broken swap.
* All 40 A/B cells in this campaign md5-identical between arms.
* `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` proven ACTIVE, not merely present: planting an
  `unsafe` block in `decode.rs` fails the build with
  `error: usage of an unsafe block`.

### Mutation proofs — every new test made to FAIL, then restored green

    mutation                                        test that failed
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    M1  delete the pixel-width guard                serial_adapts…, one_tile…
    M2b serial branch never adapts (old behaviour)  serial_adapts…
    M3  width gate leaks into concurrent branch     serial_adapts…, one_tile…
    M4  set_pixel_bytes stores instead of fetch_max set_pixel_bytes_is_monotone
    M5  block_shift() disagrees by one bit between  cross_shard_overlaps_are_all_caught
        consecutive registrations (SOUNDNESS)
    M6  drop the serial `shift.max(BLOCK_SHIFT)`    serial_adapts…
    M7  serial uses the concurrent block-count      serial_adapts…
        target

M5 is the one that matters. The adaptive shift rests on exactly one premise —
both registrants of a shared byte agree on where the block boundary is — and
this change adds a THIRD input to the value they must agree on, so the premise
was re-proven rather than inherited. Restoring it: 27/27 pass.

Soundness argument, unchanged in shape: the shift is read once in
`BorrowTracker::new`, is immutable for that tracker's life (it moves only in
`reprovision`, which takes `&mut self`), and two registrants of a shared byte
share the instance. A stale or racing latch value only decides which constant a
*new* tracker picks. That is why all three latches can be process-global and
monotone without touching correctness.


## 6. What is NOT explained, and what was NOT done

* **The mechanism behind the 10-bit fast-path penalty is measured, not
  understood.** `add`'s self time rises 3.5x more at 10bpc than at 8bpc for the
  same coarsening. Blocks-per-shard does not explain it (matching that quantity,
  10bpc shift 15, is still 1.049), and neither does bytes-per-shard (fixed by
  length and shard count, not by the shift). The shipped rule is keyed on the
  measured discriminator; a mechanism might key it better.
* **Sizes covered are 1024 and 4K only, at two depths.** 8K was not measured, so
  neither a `BD::BPC` rule nor a byte-stride rule is validated there — they
  differ exactly at "8-bit plane with a 10-bit-sized stride", which is what an
  8K 8-bit plane is. 12bpc was not measured either; it takes the 2-bytes path
  with 10bpc, which is the conservative side.
* **Shift 16 for the serial 8-bit arm was not tried adaptively.** The
  serial-specific `BLOCKS_PER_SHARD = 1` lands the 4K plane on 15, and the
  fixed ladder puts 16 within noise of 15 (0.937 vs 0.936), so there is
  probably nothing left — but a ratio of 1/2 (i.e. shift 16) was not built.
* **`ShardRecs::locs` is stored unconditionally** (`tracker_shard.rs`, in
  `alloc`) even though the doc comment on `Loc` says release builds do not keep
  it. That is one store plus a `#[track_caller]` `&Location` materialised per
  borrow — two instructions on the hottest path in the decoder, at both depths.
  Not touched here: it is in `alloc`, which the concurrent wide-path work owns.
* **`check_cast_slice_len` re-reads `self.len()`** after the tracker call. One
  load; left alone.

### Pre-existing failure found, NOT caused by this change

`cargo test --release --workspace` fails 16 of 19 `decode_permutations` tests
with `called Option::unwrap() on a None value` at
`src/safe_simd/mc_arm.rs:5931`. **Verified identical on pristine `main` in a
throwaway worktree — same 16 test names, same panic site.** Cause: that suite
ablates archmage tokens process-wide, and nine sites assume NEON is always
summonable — six `Arm64::summon().unwrap()` in `mc_arm.rs`, three in
`filmgrain_arm.rs`, plus one
`.expect("NEON always available on aarch64")` — so the scalar permutation
panics instead of dispatching scalar. `decode_md5_verify` does not ablate, which
is why the corpus gate is green either way.


## 7. Measured negatives — do not re-run these

* **A pixel-stride-keyed or rows-per-block-keyed shift: REFUTED.** It computes
  the same 14/15 the length rule already computes (section 2), so it reproduces
  the +4.9% 10-bit regression it was meant to fix.
* **A coarser block for 10-bit at t=1, at any rung: REFUTED.** 1.012 / 1.059 /
  1.049 / 1.044 at shifts 13/14/15/16 on v4k_8tile_10b, replicated on
  v4k_1tile_10b. The optimum is the base shift.
* **`#[inline(always)]` on the zerocopy casts, alone: a 2.3-2.7% REGRESSION** at
  10bpc t=1. It is only a win once the cold `CastError` frame is gone.
